  leverage the built-in behaviors of the Bash shell to construct and execute commands using only numbers and symbols.

  #### 1. Wildcard Expansion (Globbing)

  Bash supports globbing characters like ? (matches any single character) and * (matches zero or more characters). If an administrator restricts alphabetic input,
  these symbols can be used to reference paths and executables:

  • Basic Pattern: The pattern /???/??? matches any file path containing a 3-letter directory followed by a 3-letter filename (e.g., /bin/cat, /bin/pwd, /usr/bin if
  it fits).
  • Execution Behavior: When a glob pattern is entered as a command, Bash expands it alphabetically. The first matching file path in alphabetical order is treated
  as the executable, while any subsequent matches are passed to it as arguments.
      • For example, if /bin/cat is the first alphabetical match for a pattern, running that pattern with a wildcard argument might execute /bin/cat against all
      files in the current directory.


  #### 2. Parameter Slicing from Environment Variables

  Bash allows substring extraction from existing environment variables using the syntax ${VARIABLE:offset:length}. Since offsets and lengths are numeric, they do
  not require alphabetic characters.

  If certain standard variables are defined in the environment (e.g., PATH, PWD, or SHELL), they can serve as sources of letters:

  • Finding Variable Indexes: In a standard shell, variables contain predictable paths:
      • If $PATH contains /usr/bin:/bin, the character at index 0 is /.
      • If $SHELL contains /bin/bash, individual letters like b, a, s, h can be isolated by target indices:
          • ${SHELL:5:1} yields b
          • ${SHELL:6:1} yields a
          • ${SHELL:7:1} yields s
          • ${SHELL:8:1} yields h

  • Special Variables: Even in highly restricted environments, special shell parameters are automatically defined:
      • $0: Expands to the name of the shell or the shell script (often bash, -bash, or sh).
      • $_: Expands to the last argument of the previous command.


  #### 3. Reconstructing Commands

  Once individual characters or substrings are isolated via slicing, they can be concatenated directly inside the shell to form a command.

  • Example Structure: If ${0} evaluates to bash:
      • ${0:0:1} is b
      • ${0:1:1} is a
      • ${0:2:1} is s
      • ${0:3:1} is h
      • Concatenating these as ${0:0:1}${0:1:1}${0:2:1}${0:3:1} reconstructs the string bash and executes it.                                                                                                                                                
